Sliding Window Maximum

Problem Description
Given an array of integers nums and an integer k, there is a sliding window of size k moving from left to right. Return the maximum element in each sliding window position.

Example 1:
Input: nums = [1,3,-1,-3,5,3,6,7], k = 3
Output: [3,3,5,5,6,7]

Example 2:
Input: nums = [1], k = 1
Output: [1]

Intuition

Finding the maximum in each window naively takes O(k) per step, leading to O(n \cdot k) total time, which easily exceeds time limits for large inputs.

We can optimize this to O(n) using a monotonic deque (double-ended queue) that stores indices in decreasing order of their corresponding values. The front of the deque will always hold the index of the maximum value in the current window.

As we traverse the array:

Pop from the front if the index is out of the window boundary (i - k).

Pop from the back if the value at that index is less than or equal to the current element a[i], since it can no longer be the maximum.

Push the current index to the back.

Record a[q.\text{front()}] into our result array once the window reaches size k.

Approach

Monotonic Deque Maintenance:
Initialize a deque to store element indices and a vector res for the window maximums.
Iterate through array a with index i from 0 to n - 1:

If q is not empty and q.\text{front}() == i - k, remove the front index as it has slipped out of the window.

While $q$ is not empty and a[q.\text{back}()] \le a[i], pop from the back to maintain a monotonically decreasing queue.

Push index $i$ to the back of q

Once i \ge k - 1, append a[q.\text{front}()] to res.

Complexity Analysis:
Time Complexity: O(n)
Every element is added to and removed from the deque at most once across the entire loop.
Space Complexity: O(k) Auxiliary Space
The deque stores at most k indices at any given point.

Code (Clean & Modular C++)

#include <vector>
#include <deque>

using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> maxSlidingWindow(vector<int>& a, int k) {
        deque<int> q;
        vector<int> res;
        for (int i = 0; i < a.size(); ++i) {
            if (!q.empty() && q.front() == i - k) q.pop_front();
            while (!q.empty() && a[q.back()] <= a[i]) q.pop_back();
            q.push_back(i);
            if (i >= k - 1) res.push_back(a[q.front()]);
        }
        return res;
    }
};
